WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.902 --> 00:00:11.688
imece runs a team of coding agents from many vendors on one codebase, on one screen.

2
00:00:11.688 --> 00:00:18.312
A leader reads your checkout and hires workers as the work needs them; each worker

3
00:00:18.312 --> 00:00:22.755
changes a git worktree of its own, never your checkout.

4
00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:26.633
Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI, side by side.

5
00:00:27.835 --> 00:00:29.262
This is the salon.

6
00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.749
Each project is a group with its own leader.

7
00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:39.408
A worker's row shows its card and what it is doing right now; the mark in front says

8
00:00:39.408 --> 00:00:44.085
where it stands: working, starting, waiting for you, stuck.

9
00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:50.743
The small bars show how busy it was in the last half minute, the three lines how far

10
00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:55.182
it is through its own to-do list, then what it has cost.

11
00:00:55.182 --> 00:01:01.206
The journal on the right keeps every message and every event, with the time.

12
00:01:02.407 --> 00:01:09.956
Enter looks deeper: an agent's screen, with how full its context is, its to-do list

13
00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:13.139
step by step, and its card's story.

14
00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:18.505
Enter again shows the raw stream from the vendor's own CLI.

15
00:01:18.505 --> 00:01:20.051
Escape goes back.

16
00:01:20.051 --> 00:01:26.418
Three depths, everywhere: the guild, one card's story, the raw stream.

17
00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:33.979
Agents talk to each other over a bus: they ask, answer, hand work off, report

18
00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:37.283
progress, and say when they are blocked.

19
00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:42.322
imece reads only the envelope; the body is never interpreted.

20
00:01:42.322 --> 00:01:48.270
Let us speed the simulation up, let them work for a while, and pause it.

21
00:01:49.854 --> 00:01:54.035
Everything that needs you lands in one place: WAITING FOR YOU.

22
00:01:54.035 --> 00:01:59.563
A change a worker has finished, a command or an edit a vendor would have asked you

23
00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:01.316
about, a question for you.

24
00:02:01.316 --> 00:02:06.306
The terminal's title counts them, so a tab in the background shows it too.

25
00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:13.684
Capital W opens the next item from anywhere.

26
00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:20.327
This one is a change: what the worker's worktree holds since its last done.

27
00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:26.263
The files above the patch, the lines by kind, then the diff itself.

28
00:02:26.263 --> 00:02:30.957
j and k scroll it; the brackets go from file to file.

29
00:02:32.159 --> 00:02:37.987
a applies the change to your checkout, staged, for you to look over and commit.

30
00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:42.045
r sends it back and opens the composer, so you say why.

31
00:02:42.045 --> 00:02:48.020
Git does its work on a thread of its own; the journal says when the change is in.

32
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.930
Your checkout is the one place an agent never writes.

33
00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:55.477
Let the simulation run again.

34
00:02:55.477 --> 00:02:59.197
One pot for the whole team, kept between runs.

35
00:02:59.197 --> 00:03:05.749
The header shows what was spent, what the last ten minutes cost per hour, and how

36
00:03:05.749 --> 00:03:08.338
long the pot lasts at that rate.

37
00:03:08.338 --> 00:03:15.051
Beside it, each vendor plan's usage window nearest to full, and the fleet: working,

38
00:03:15.051 --> 00:03:16.022
done, stuck.

39
00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:24.538
Two is the board: the cards the leaders posted, who claimed which, what is done.

40
00:03:24.538 --> 00:03:28.469
An idle worker may claim a card on its own.

41
00:03:28.469 --> 00:03:34.778
Three is the code map: one row per file, and who holds which symbols.

42
00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:37.155
One brings the salon back.

43
00:03:38.355 --> 00:03:41.679
You talk to the leader, not to thirty agents.

44
00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:47.735
Type what you want; the leader turns it into cards, and the workers take them from

45
00:03:47.735 --> 00:03:48.474
the board.

46
00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:52.167
The reply comes back as something waiting for you.

47
00:03:53.368 --> 00:04:01.742
q asks before quitting: how many agents are mid-turn, how many changes you have not

48
00:04:01.742 --> 00:04:02.650
reviewed.

49
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.961
s lists them.

50
00:04:03.961 --> 00:04:12.335
Everything imece knows is kept in one SQLite file; the next imece opens on the same

51
00:04:12.335 --> 00:04:17.379
screen, every agent asleep until you write to one.

52
00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:20.253
imece is open source.

53
00:04:20.253 --> 00:04:24.872
Install it with one line, and try the simulation for free.

